2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note THE LOAD-BEARING CLAIM, observed with ORT verbose logging (`env.logLevel = LogLevel.DEBUG`), not merely a success/failure probe: `device:'auto'` on Linux x64 resolves (`src/backends/onnx.js`) to the execution-provider list `['cuda','webgpu','cpu']`, in that order, and ORT registers all three as fallback providers in one session. For `Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2` at q8, graph partitioning placed 340 of 402 nodes on CUDAExecutionProvider and the remaining 62 (two ORT cost-heuristic CPU-preferred nodes, plus every DynamicQuantizeLinear/MatMulInteger node — q8's quantization ops, for which CUDA registers no kernel) on CPUExecutionProvider; WebGpuExecutionProvider was initialized (a Dawn device context was created, hence the "maxDynamicUniformBuffersPerPipelineLayout" warnings seen on every run) but received ZERO node placements — inert for this model. So on this host `auto` genuinely runs on the GPU, not a silent CPU fallback, with per-node CPU offload for ops CUDA cannot serve.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note THAT RESULT DOES NOT GENERALISE. For `granite-97m-multilingual-r2` (registry id) at fp32, `auto` reproducibly CRASHES mid-execution (exit -6/SIGABRT, no output): "Non-zero status code returned while running Sqrt node… All inputs must be tensors on WebGPU buffers" — ORT's partitioner assigned this model's Sqrt node to WebGpuExecutionProvider while feeding it a CPU-resident tensor, a genuine mixed-CUDA/WebGPU-partitioning bug in this ORT build, not a benign fallback. Same model, explicit `device:'cuda'` (no WebGPU registered at all) loads and measures cleanly (load_ms 1940.3, RSS delta 907.4 MB, query_ms_median 3.3). So `auto`'s GPU-bearing provider list is real and the failure IS caught — the process does not silently serve CPU-only or hang — but the failure is a hard crash for at least one candidate, not a graceful same-session fallback. Recorded as a first-class result per the ticket's own instruction.
