2026-08-30T10:20Z claude note ACTION 8, FULL-CORPUS WALL CLOCK — the headline finding. A literal 93 022-row run at batch 32 (device auto, q8) for `arctic-embed-m-v2` measured 177 ms/passage over the first 9 280 rows (partial run, terminated for time after confirming the rate; verification/DEVICE-AUTO-0264.md's per-node placement finding explains why this is GPU work, not a silent CPU fallback). That rate matches, within 8%, the SAME model's regular 600-row fidelity measurement at batch 8 (164 ms/passage) — the same rate at 15x the sample size and 4x the batch, which is why the full-corpus figures below are PROJECTED from the 600-row measurements rather than each fully re-run: every candidate's fidelity cells already give a same-stack, same-driver, same-device per-passage rate, and the one direct larger-scale check confirms it is stable rather than an artifact of the small sample. Every candidate, every dtype, in the SAME reduced range: 58–256 ms/passage — projecting to 89–396 MINUTES for the full 93 022-passage corpus, not the "minutes" this ticket's own body anticipated, and one to two orders of magnitude slower than the superseded PyTorch-derived gpu-feasibility.json (7–95 minutes projected for a different, English-only field). Quantization is NOT the explanation: fp32 (no quantization ops at all) is exactly as slow as q8/uint8 for every candidate, ruling out the CPU-fallback-kernel hypothesis this ticket's own device-shape finding would have suggested. Single-query batch-1 latency (the cost-cell figures, a few ms) is 10–40x faster per passage than sustained large-batch throughput — sustained throughput is the bottleneck, not per-call overhead. Cause not established; reported as the observation, not a diagnosed defect. R30's wall-clock bound is NOT pinned in this change: DECISIONS.md frames the pin as landing "in the same change that first asserts it," but the surviving-candidate choice is ticket 0267's (blocked on this one) and the throughput anomaly above is unexplained — pinning a number now would either lock in the wrong candidate's figure or paper over a possibly-fixable performance defect. Deferred to 0267 with these figures as its input; flagged prominently rather than silently left stale.
2026-08-30T10:20Z claude note ACTION 7, X8 CROSS-PROVIDER FIDELITY — DESIGN §3's question, answered. CPU-side vectors (0263, merged) at /home/haduong/data/projets/zoteus-bench/0263-vectors/ scored against this arm's GPU vectors, same (model, rung), same 600 rows: bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/x8-cross-provider-fidelity.json. Every one of 18 scored (model, rung) pairs — all 6 surviving candidates at fp32/q8/uint8, the three rungs both arms share — returns cosine 1,000000, clearing DESIGN §3's 0,999 bar with no exception. Verified past the scorer, by hand: the CPU-side and GPU-side `.f32` files are byte-identical (sha256/md5 match, correct sizes) for every sampled pair, not merely numerically close. Per DESIGN §3's rule, the execution provider stays OUT of the embedder key for every surviving candidate, and the adopt-a-foreign-index question in DECISIONS.md is settled on the evidence: an index embedded on padme (GPU) can serve queries embedded on doudou (CPU) with zero measured fidelity loss, for fp32/q8/uint8. fp16 is excluded from this verdict by construction (DESIGN §3's own carve-out: the CPU provider cannot load it at all, so no CPU query-side embedder can match an fp16-embedded corpus regardless of X8's outcome).
