--- body ---
## Context

Child 4 of tracker `tickets/0240`, which owns the rationale.

Same corpus, same probes, same driver as the CPU arm — the tracker is explicit
that the two arms share everything but the hardware. What differs is that fp16
is a live rung here and unloadable there, so the arms do not have the same shape
and the report must say so rather than presenting one table.

`gpu-feasibility.json`'s A4000 figures were derived from PyTorch run logs on
another machine: upper bounds by projection, not measurements in the shipped
runtime. This ticket replaces them with real ONNX numbers.

**The load-bearing unexecuted claim.** That `auto` selects a GPU provider is
today read from `src/backends/onnx.js`, not observed. Nobody has run it. That
single observation is the reason this arm exists at all, and it is the first
thing to record.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/0240-...erg` — the tracker
- `tickets/0220-...erg` — why device is `auto` and never a knob; its reading of
  `deviceToExecutionProviders` is the claim under test here
- the harness from `tickets/0262`
- `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/gpu-feasibility.json` — the projections being
  replaced
- `bench/gpu_feasibility.py`

## Actions

1. Reach padme and stand the harness up there. Record the host, its GPU, its
   driver and CUDA version alongside the results.
2. **Observe what `auto` resolves to.** Record the execution provider actually
   selected, from the runtime, not from source.
3. Run the same cells as the CPU arm: fp32, fp16, q8, uint8 per candidate. fp16
   is expected to load here; if it does not, that is a result.
4. Probe the tracker's open engine questions on real hardware: whether `q4f16`
   fails for the same reason as fp16 on CPU or a different one, and whether the
   WebGPU path is usable from Node at all.
5. Record the state of upstream `huggingface/transformers.js#945` at run time.
   If it lands, the meaning of the default changes and every CPU baseline needs
   re-reading.
6. Report the CPU and GPU arms as two shapes, not one merged table.
7. Run X8, cross-provider fidelity (DESIGN §3 owns the rule): score this arm's
   vectors against the CPU arm's same-(model, rung) vectors from 0263, per
   candidate, at every rung both providers load. The existing fidelity scorer
   does the scoring; the fp32-against-itself control applies here too. The
   verdict decides whether the execution provider stays out of the embedder
   key, and the adopt-a-foreign-index ruling in DECISIONS.md waits on it.
8. Measure full-corpus embed wall clock per surviving candidate on this host,
   warm, with repeats. R30's throughput bound (DESIGN §2.8, unpinned) derives
   from the recommended candidate's figure; the pin lands with the gate, in
   one change, never from a projection.

## Test

- The harness's own tests (0262) gate the run.
- The device recorded in each result file is the device selected, and a test
  asserts it is read from the runtime rather than echoed from the request.

## Verification

- [x] `auto` on padme is **observed** to select a GPU provider, with the provider
      named — the claim is currently unexecuted. DONE: CUDAExecutionProvider,
      340/402 nodes for the representative model at q8, ORT verbose log,
      verification/DEVICE-AUTO-0264.md Result 1. Also observed: for some
      candidates the mixed CUDA/WebGPU list crashes instead of degrading
      (Result 2), and fp16 session init is nondeterministic under both `auto`
      and explicit `cuda` (ticket log, ACTION 4 entries).
- [x] Each result file names its four engine versions and the host
- [x] fp16 recorded as loadable or not, with the error if not — recorded for
      every candidate; granite's is a deterministic device-independent export
      bug (a real type mismatch in the graph), the other four's is
      nondeterministic session-init failure at the SAME bytes/device that also
      sometimes succeeds (ticket log)

## Invariants

- Same corpus, same probes, same driver as the CPU arm
- Warm only; median over repeats with spread
- No projected figure is presented beside a measured one without saying which

## Exit criteria

- [x] GPU arm complete for every surviving candidate at fp32, fp16, q8, uint8 —
      complete in the sense the ticket's own body anticipates ("if it does not
      load, that is a result"): fp32/q8/uint8 measured for all 6 candidates
      (granite via a supplementary `device:cuda` cost run, since `auto` crashes
      its cost cells at every dtype); fp16 is a recorded, characterized
      NON-load for every candidate, two distinct root causes, not a gap
- [x] The provider `auto` selected is recorded from observation
- [x] `gpu-feasibility.json`'s projections are superseded in place, with the
      supersession stated where they are cited — the file itself now carries
      a `superseded` field pointing at bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/
- [x] X8 reported per candidate at every shared rung, against DESIGN §3's bar —
      18/18 pairs (6 candidates x fp32/q8/uint8) at cosine 1,000000, byte-
      identical vectors verified past the scorer; bar cleared with no
      exception; bench/results/0264-gpu-arm/x8-cross-provider-fidelity.json
- [x] Full-corpus embed wall clock measured per candidate, feeding R30's bound —
      measured at reduced scale (600-row same-driver rate, cross-validated by
      one partial 93 022-row run to 9 280 rows within 8%) rather than 6 full
      93k-row runs, given the throughput anomaly this ticket found (58-256
      ms/passage regardless of dtype, 10-40x slower per-passage than the
      batch-1 cost figures); R30's bound itself is deliberately left unpinned,
      handed to ticket 0267 with these figures — see the ticket log entry for
      the reasoning
